# Build output
bin/
obj/
artifacts/
*.user
.vs/
.idea/

# Never commit game assets. Perianth reads user-supplied files; none of them
# belong in this repository, and several are copyrighted.
*.mmb
*.cameldata
*.editordata
*.anim
*.mlipsyncdatabase
*.dds
*.wem
*.wav
*.glb
*.gltf
*.sdfdata
*.sdftoc
*.mshader

# The rest of what the archives ship. The list above was written when the tool
# read eight formats; it now reads, writes or extracts most of these, and an
# extraction writes a tree of whatever the author asked for. Taken from the
# shipped archives themselves rather than from what the code names, so a format
# nothing here parses yet is still refused entry.
#
# Deliberately absent are the extensions a source repository legitimately uses
# — .json, .txt, .xml, .csv, .html, .css, .bat, .cfg, .dat, .bin, .h, .map —
# even though the archives hold files with those names too. An ignore rule that
# hides a file somebody meant to commit is worse than one that misses a file the
# content scan will catch.
*.fruit
*.juice
*.locpack
*.locpackbin
*.manimevent
*.manimsync
*.manimsys
*.mbakedlayers
*.mbehavior
*.mcache
*.mcelllogic
*.mcompoundnode
*.mcoonstagram
*.mdependencyrules
*.mdestructionstate
*.menvironment
*.mgraphobject
*.mitem
*.mitemcomponenttype
*.mkeybinds
*.mknockbackchainmultiplier
*.mlapdance
*.mlayer
*.mlevel
*.mlevelbasedcombatlookuptable
*.mleveltemplate
*.mlocationeffect
*.mma
*.mmission
*.mnpc
*.mparticles
*.mphys
*.mplayer
*.mpower
*.mprimarystats
*.mprojectile
*.mrte
*.mscript
*.msequence
*.mshadergraph
*.mshaders
*.mskillscriptgraph
*.mskillscriptjuice
*.mticket
*.muigraph
*.muiprefab
*.mvar
*.mvendorconfig
*.mwidget
*.opencl
*.pngcache
*.prefabs
*.seqcam
*.sounddata
*.spritesheet

# Media and fonts the archives ship. Force-add with `git add -f` if this
# repository ever wants an icon or a font of its own; nothing does today, and
# an extracted texture landing here silently is the failure worth preventing.
*.bik
*.bk2
*.bnk
*.fontmetrics
*.otf
*.png
*.psd
*.tga
*.tif
*.ttf
*.sdfver

# What this tool's own commands leave behind. An extraction mirrors the
# archive's paths, so it writes a `camel/` tree wherever it is run — including
# here, when a working directory is not what it was assumed to be. That has
# happened.
/camel/
perianth-extraction.json
manifest.ini
